# Citation metadata of walkbench (Citation File Format 1.2.0).
# Written by the SP1.1 session of SIM-ACI (Claude Opus 5.5) on 2026-11-12, without a
# code-generation model. Title, version and abstract follow DESCRIPTION; the abstract
# writes each DOI as "doi:..." in the parentheses of its citation, and its last sentence,
# on the user guide, was added on 2026-11-18 by the same session, completed on
# 2026-11-20 for version 0.7.0 (folder web/) and revised on 2026-11-21 (guide of
# version 0.7.0). The field doi is the
# concept DOI of walkbench on Zenodo, which names all the versions; the reference of the
# preprint of the first article of SIM-ACI was added the same day. The license of the
# guide (CC BY 4.0) is stated in README.md and .zenodo.json, because CFF reads several
# licenses as alternatives. When .zenodo.json is
# present, Zenodo uses that file only; the two files give the same title, author,
# version, license, keywords and description. Copyright (c) 2026 Philippe Terrier.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use walkbench, please cite it as below. The Zenodo record of each release gives the DOI of that version."
title: "walkbench: Testbed of Walking Models for the Divergence Exponents of Gait"
type: software
version: "0.7.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24354488"
authors:
  - family-names: Terrier
    given-names: Philippe
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3693-4505"
    affiliation: "Haute Ecole Arc Santé, HES-SO University of Applied Sciences and Arts Western Switzerland, Neuchâtel, Switzerland"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Ph-Terrier/walkbench"
abstract: >
  Walking models share a common interface, so that an experiment written for
  one model runs on the others without change. The interface has a
  perturbation port that accepts any input series, a closed-loop port for
  corrective controllers and a metronome, and a linearization at the fixed
  point. Runs have standardized outputs, and a run driver applies a seed rule,
  redraws a run after a fall and writes a run log. Common estimators compute
  the short-term and long-term divergence exponents with the Rosenstein engine
  of RACING v1.0.2 (Terrier 2026, doi:10.5281/zenodo.20542521), the Floquet
  multipliers, the kinematic variability and the scaling exponent of detrended
  fluctuation analysis. The package contains two models. M1 is the simplest
  walking model of Garcia et al. (1998, doi:10.1115/1.2798313), perturbed at
  each foot strike as in Su and Dingwell (2007, doi:10.1115/1.2800760). M2 is
  the walking model with arced feet and a hip spring of Hobbelen and Wisse
  (2007, doi:10.1109/TRO.2007.904908), as used by Bruijn et al. (2012,
  doi:10.1016/j.medengphy.2011.07.024). Its input is the height of the floor
  at each step. The campaign rules follow the analysis plan of the SIM-ACI
  project, registered on OSF Registries (doi:10.17605/OSF.IO/M74TK). They cover the
  master seeds, the transients, the rescaling of the inputs, the real stride
  series and their surrogates, and the fall rate of each cell. An analytic
  predictor gives the expected divergence curve and the long-term exponent
  from the linearization of a model and the autocovariance of its input.
  The folder docs/ of the repository holds the user guide of version 0.7.0 in
  PDF and the example scripts that produced it. The folder web/ holds a
  simulator of the models M1 and M2 for a web browser.
keywords:
  - passive dynamic walking
  - walking model
  - gait variability
  - local dynamic stability
  - divergence exponent
  - attractor complexity index
  - long-range correlations
  - simulation
  - R
